talk(401,'1976-09-13','D. B. Fuchs','P000663-P000664','The Weil algebra and its differential','talk',
 r'''Fuchs constructs the graded algebra \(\Lambda\mathfrak g^*\otimes S\mathfrak g^*\), specifies its differential on generators, explains the signed Leibniz rule and gives the structure-constant formula on the symmetric generators. The concluding acyclicity statement omits the surviving degree-zero constants. Characteristic classes are announced for a future meeting, not developed here. This is established Weil--Cartan theory: \citet{cartan1950weil,cartan1950transgression}. The lecture begins on P000663 and ends before the explicit Kirillov intervention on P000664.''',catalogue_pages='P000662-P000665')
talk(402,'1976-09-13','A. A. Kirillov','P000664','Transgression and invariant forms on compact Lie groups','talk',
 r'''A short intervention explains the purpose of the Weil algebra in computing compact Lie-group cohomology. The intended correspondence is between primitive odd cohomology generators and indecomposable symmetric invariant polynomials. The notebook states a stronger, false dimension equality for all invariant forms and reverses the differential direction in its transgression shorthand. The surrounding Fuchs and Arnold lectures belong to separate records.''',catalogue_pages='P000663-P000665')
item(401,'P000663','definition','The graded Weil algebra',
 r'''\(W(\mathfrak g)=\Lambda^*(\mathfrak g')\otimes S^*(\mathfrak g')\), viewed as functions of \(2n\) variables, with \(\Lambda^p\otimes S^q\) in degree \(p+2q\).''',
 r'''For a finite-dimensional Lie algebra \(\mathfrak g\) over \(\mathbb R\) or \(\mathbb C\), \(W(\mathfrak g)\) is the free graded-commutative algebra on odd generators \(\theta^i\) of cohomological degree1 and even generators \(u^i\) of degree2, two copies of \(\mathfrak g^*\). Thus its underlying superdimension is \(n|n\); ``functions'' here means polynomials in the even variables and exterior polynomials in the odd ones.''',
 r'''The field, finite-dimensionality and polynomial interpretation are supplied. No smooth or completed function algebra is implied.''','E',
 r'''The construction is already in \citet[Expos\'e19, Weil-algebra construction]{cartan1950weil}, delivered15May1950.''',
 r'''Standard definition. The earlier discussion of superfunctions in1975-389.1 supplies context but is not the same construction.''',['cartan1950weil'],literal='correct',area='Lie algebra cohomology and topology')
item(401,'P000663','construction','The Chevalley differential sign convention',
 r'''For \(h\in\Lambda^\ell\mathfrak g^*\), the displayed sum is \(\sum_{i<j}(-1)^{i+j-1}h([g_i,g_j],g_1,\ldots,\widehat g_i,\ldots,\widehat g_j,\ldots)\); in degree1 it is \(h([g_1,g_2])\).''',
 r'''With one-based argument indices, define \(d_+h\) by exactly that alternating sum. It is the negative of the frequently used trivial-coefficient Chevalley--Eilenberg differential, so \(d_+^2=0\) by Jacobi and it obeys the graded Leibniz rule. Either overall sign convention computes the same cohomology.''',
 r'''The remaining arguments retain their original order after the two omissions. The positive bracket sign is internally consistent and is not a transcription error.''','E',
 r'''Compare the exterior differential within the pre-existing Weil construction of \citet[Expos\'e19]{cartan1950weil}; the overall sign is conventional.''',
 r'''Correct with the stated indexing and sign convention.''',['cartan1950weil'],literal='correct',area='Lie algebra cohomology and topology')
item(401,'P000663-P000664','construction','The unique Weil differential on both sets of generators',
 r'''The differential sends \(h\otimes1\) to \((d_+h)\otimes1+1\otimes h\), is a graded derivation, and is said to extend uniquely with square zero. P000664 gives \(d(1\otimes h^k)=c_{ij}^{k}(h^i\otimes h^j)\).''',
 r'''If \([e_i,e_j]=c_{ij}^{k}e_k\), the notebook's sign convention gives \[d\theta^k=\tfrac12c_{ij}^{k}\theta^i\theta^j+u^k,\qquad du^k=c_{ij}^{k}\theta^iu^j.\] Together with \(d(ab)=d(a)b+(-1)^{\deg a}a\,d(b)\), these formulas define a unique degree-one derivation satisfying \(d^2=0\). The second formula is forced by \(d^2\theta^k=0\); Jacobi then gives \(d^2u^k=0\).''',
 r'''\ours\ The factor \(1/2\) arises from summing over all ordered pairs \(i,j\); it disappears if one sums only \(i<j\). We have checked the sign of \(du\) against the notebook's positive exterior differential.''','E',
 r'''This is the Weil differential of \citet[Expos\'e19]{cartan1950weil}, with an explicitly reconciled sign convention, not a new1976 construction.''',
 r'''Correct. The two tensor factors in the structure-constant formula have degrees1 and2, respectively.''',['cartan1950weil'],literal='correct',area='Lie algebra cohomology and topology')
item(401,'P000664','theorem','Acyclicity in positive degree, with constants surviving',
 r'''The ``main theorem'' says that the homologies of the complex are zero, without a qualification on degree.''',
 r'''For the unaugmented cochain algebra above, \(H^0(W(\mathfrak g),d)=\mathbb K\) and \(H^j(W(\mathfrak g),d)=0\) for \(j>0\). Equivalently, the augmentation \(W(\mathfrak g)\to\mathbb K\) is a quasi-isomorphism.''',
 r'''\ours\ Put \(v^i=d\theta^i\). This is an invertible triangular replacement of the even generators; now \(d\theta^i=v^i\), \(dv^i=0\). The odd operator \(h=\sum_i\theta^i\partial/\partial v^i\) satisfies \(dh+hd=N\), where \(N\) counts the total number of generators. Division by positive \(N\) contracts every positive-weight component, but not the constant1.''','E',
 r'''\citet[Expos\'e20, \S2, Theorem1]{cartan1950transgression} states the precise acyclicity result in1950.''',
 r'''The positive-degree theorem is established; literal vanishing in degree0 is false. The notebook also says ``homologies'' although its differential raises degree.''',['cartan1950transgression'],literal='false as written',match='partial',area='Lie algebra cohomology and topology',development='annotated')
item(402,'P000664','method','Computing compact Lie-group cohomology from invariant forms',
 r'''Kirillov says that the algebra was devised for computing the homologies of simple compact Lie groups, and that invariant symmetric forms are easier to find than skew-symmetric ones.''',
 r'''For a compact connected Lie group \(K\) over \(\mathbb R\), averaging identifies de Rham cohomology with the cohomology of invariant forms; bi-invariant forms give the harmonic representatives for a bi-invariant metric. The cohomology algebra is exterior on primitive odd-degree generators. Invariant polynomials \((S\mathfrak k^*)^K\), together with transgression, determine their degrees. Simplicity is not needed for this real-coefficient statement.''',
 r'''Connectedness, compactness and real coefficients are explicit. This does not assert an integral-cohomology computation, where torsion is invisible to differential forms.''','E',
 r'''\citet[Expos\'e20, \S\S3--4]{cartan1950transgression} supplies the transgression method before1976.''',
 r'''Established real-coefficient theory. The method statement is distinct from the incorrect unrestricted counting rule in the next occurrence.''',['cartan1950transgression'],area='Lie algebra cohomology and topology')
item(402,'P000664','claim','Primitive odd forms versus indecomposable symmetric invariants',
 r'''The conclusion reads: as many invariant skew-symmetric forms of degree \(k\) as symmetric ones of degree \((k+1)/2\). A preceding sentence calls an odd form the differential of a symmetric expression.''',
 r'''Let \(I=(S\mathfrak k^*)^K\), \(I^+\) its positive-degree ideal, and \(Q(I)=I^+/(I^+)^2\). For compact connected \(K\), transgression identifies \(Q(I)_m\) with the primitive part of \(H^{2m-1}(K;\mathbb R)\), up to the usual choice of generators. A degree-\(m\) invariant polynomial has Weil degree \(2m\) and is \(d\) of a transgression element of degree \(2m-1\), whose exterior component represents the corresponding primitive class. The differential direction in the notebook must therefore be reversed.''',
 r'''\ours\ For \(K=SU(2)\), the square of the Killing quadratic polynomial is a nonzero invariant symmetric polynomial of degree4, whereas \(\Lambda^7\mathfrak{su}(2)^*=0\). This disproves the literal count at \(k=7\), while its indecomposable version is correct.''','E',
 r'''The corrected generator correspondence is \citet[Expos\'e20, \S4, Theorem2]{cartan1950transgression}.''',
 r'''Established for primitive and indecomposable generators. The unrestricted equality and the displayed differential direction are false as written.''',['cartan1950transgression'],literal='false as written',match='partial',area='Lie algebra cohomology and topology',development='annotated')
talk(403,'1976-09-13','V. I. Arnold','P000664-P000666','Point equivalence of second-order equations and projective geometry','talk',
 r'''Arnold explicitly revisits nineteenth-century work of Lie, Tresse and Liouville and Cartan's1924 projective connections. The lecture separates point linearization, pairs of line fields in a contact three-manifold, projective parameters along solutions, and inequivalent projective structures on a circle. It then normalizes the quadratic transverse terms, obtains a relative5/2-density, and constructs projective scalar invariants from its reciprocal power. A damaged global question and Gelfand's proposed cohomological connection are recorded without inventing missing quantifiers. The local normalization calculations can be checked, but the stated flatness implication needs additional hypotheses.''',catalogue_pages='P000663-P000668')
lin_id=item(403,'P000664','question','When can a scalar second-order ODE be straightened by point transformations?',
 r'''For \(y''=F(x,y,y')\), Arnold asks whether solution curves can be straightened. The page explicitly cites Tresse1896 and Acta Mathematica1894, and contrasts general equations with \(y''=0\).''',
 r'''For a smooth scalar equation on an open set in \((x,y,p)\), local equivalence under a nonsingular point change \((x,y)\mapsto(X,Y)\), on a chart where \(dX/dx\ne0\), to \(Y_{XX}=0\) holds exactly when the two relative invariants vanish identically there: \[I=F_{pppp},\quad H=D^2F_{pp}-4DF_{yp}-F_pDF_{pp}+6F_{yy}-3F_yF_{pp}+4F_pF_{yp},\qquad D=\partial_x+p\partial_y+F\partial_p.\] Vanishing at one point is insufficient; these are local identities on an open set.''',
 r'''The displayed criterion and smooth regular setting are modern precision supplied from the classical equivalence theory, not formulas written on P000664.''','E',
 r'''\citet[\S2, \S4.1]{kruglikov2009tresse} reconstructs the Lie--Liouville--Tresse criterion and its nineteenth-century provenance. Cartan's1924 treatment reaches the same flatness test geometrically \citep[\S VIII, no.24]{cartan1924projective}.''',
 r'''The local point-linearization problem is solved. The2008 preprint/2009 exposition is not the first circulation of the classical result.''',['kruglikov2009tresse','cartan1924projective'],literal='correct',problem='GP-1976-03',area='Differential equations and projective geometry')
item(403,'P000664','theorem','Every linear second-order equation is locally point-flat',
 r'''The notes state that \(y''+a(x)y'+b(x)y=0\) becomes \(y''=0\) after a change of independent variable and a change linear in \(y\); configuration theorems such as Desargues are then transferred to the solution family.''',
 r'''Let \(a,b\) be smooth on an interval and let \(u,v\) be independent solutions, with \(v\ne0\) on a smaller interval. Then \(X=u/v\), \(Y=y/v\) is a local point diffeomorphism because \(X'=W/v^2\ne0\), and every solution becomes \(Y=c_1X+c_2\). Any incidence theorem formulated for straight-line configurations transfers to configurations lying wholly in this coordinate neighbourhood; no global straightening across zeros or monodromy is asserted.''',
 r'''\ours\ The ratio-of-solutions proof also states the actual domain restriction. The doubtful Russian phrase translated ``linear menu'' is not used to infer extra content.''','E',
 r'''This classical fact is recalled in \citet[introduction]{kruglikov2009tresse}; Arnold presents it explicitly as old material.''',
 r'''Completely established locally. The incidence-theorem remark is a consequence of transporting points and curves by this diffeomorphism.''',['kruglikov2009tresse'],literal='correct',area='Differential equations and projective geometry')
item(403,'P000665','construction','Cartan geometry of a general second-order equation',
 r'''Cartan1924--25 is invoked: solutions are geodesics of a projective connection. The next lines pass to the space of line elements \((x,y,y')\).''',
 r'''A regular second-order equation defines a path geometry on the three-dimensional contact-element space and has a canonical normal Cartan connection of projective path-geometry type. It descends to an ordinary projective equivalence class of torsion-free affine connections on the two-dimensional point space precisely when \(F\) is cubic or lower in \(p=y'\). Thus a projective connection on contact elements and one on points are different notions.''',
 r'''We preserve the passage to contact elements rather than automatically declaring the general-\(F\) sentence false. The surface-connection reading requires the cubic condition recorded later on P000666.''','E',
 r'''\citet[introduction and \S\S VII--VIII, especially nos.22--24]{cartan1924projective} explicitly distinguishes the two geometries and proves the general contact-element construction.''',
 r'''Established; the distinction prevents importing an affine-geodesic claim for arbitrary \(F\).''',['cartan1924projective'],match='exact',area='Differential equations and projective geometry')
item(403,'P000665','construction','Two transverse line fields spanning a contact distribution',
 r'''Arnold says that reducing a pair of direction fields in \(\mathbb R^3\) under diffeomorphisms is equivalent to the ODE problem, then remarks that their span is a contact structure.''',
 r'''Let \(L,V\) be an ordered pair of smooth rank-one distributions on a three-manifold, transverse at the point, with \(L\oplus V\) contact. Locally choose contact coordinates \((x,y,p)\) in which \(V=\langle\partial_p\rangle\) and \(L=\langle\partial_x+p\partial_y+F\partial_p\rangle\). Local diffeomorphisms preserving the ordered pair correspond to point transformations on the local leaf space of \(V\), with their prolongations. Arbitrary pairs whose span is integrable are excluded.''',
 r'''The rank, ordering, contact and local leaf-space assumptions are restored explicitly. Exchanging the two fields gives the dual equation rather than the same point-equivalence problem.''','E',
 r'''The contact-element formulation and duality are in \citet[\S VII and \S VIII, no.23]{cartan1924projective}.''',
 r'''The regular contact version is exact. Without the contact hypothesis the announced equivalence is false.''',['cartan1924projective'],area='Contact and projective geometry')
item(403,'P000665-P000666','construction','A projective parameter along each solution',
 r'''After choosing one solution as \(y=0\), the linear part is straightened by \(\widetilde x=f(x)\), \(\widetilde y=g(x)y\). The function \(f\) satisfies a Schwarz-type equation, and a projective structure is said to arise on every solution.''',
 r'''Along a smooth solution \(y_0\), infinitesimal transverse variations satisfy \(\eta''=F_p(x,y_0,y_0')\eta'+F_y(x,y_0,y_0')\eta\). If \(u,v\) are independent Jacobi solutions, \([u(x):v(x)]\in\mathbb{RP}^1\) gives a local projective coordinate; changing the Jacobi basis changes it by a Möbius map. For \(\eta''+a\eta'+b\eta=0\), its affine ratio satisfies \(S(f)=2b-a'-a^2/2\), where \(S(f)=f^{(3)}/f'-\tfrac32(f''/f')^2\).''',
 r'''\ours\ Removing the first derivative by \(\eta=e^{-\frac12\int a}z\) gives \(z''+(b-a'/2-a^2/4)z=0\); the ratio identity proves the Schwarzian formula. The construction is local along a nonsingular solution.''','E',
 r'''This is the projective parametrization inherent in the classical connection theory \citep[\S\S VII--VIII]{cartan1924projective}; the notebook explains it through linearization rather than a new existence theorem.''',
 r'''Established locally. Global holonomy and the topology of closed solutions are additional data, not determined by this local construction.''',['cartan1924projective'],literal='correct',match='partial',area='Differential equations and projective geometry')
item(403,'P000665','construction','Inequivalent projective structures on the circle',
 r'''The standard \(\mathbb{RP}^1\) and its pullback under a degree-two covering \(S^1\to\mathbb{RP}^1\) are called inequivalent. Arnold proposes examining infinitesimal automorphisms \(\partial_x,x\partial_x,x^2\partial_x\).''',
 r'''For each positive integer \(n\), pull back the projective atlas on \(\mathbb{RP}^1\) by the degree-\(n\) covering. The resulting circle structures are pairwise inequivalent under projective-structure-preserving diffeomorphisms. In an angular coordinate their infinitesimal projective automorphism fields are spanned by \(\partial_\theta,\sin(n\theta)\partial_\theta,\cos(n\theta)\partial_\theta\). A nonzero such field can have at most \(2n\) distinct zeros, and the maximum is achieved.''',
 r'''\ours\ The zero-count maximum is preserved by diffeomorphisms, distinguishing \(n=1\) from \(n=2\) as the notebook requests. The abstract Lie algebra is \(\mathfrak{sl}_2\) in both cases; its realization on the circle, not its abstract isomorphism type, distinguishes them.''','E',
 r'''This is an elementary consequence of the classical projective action; \citet[projective frame construction]{cartan1924projective} supplies the historical framework. No complete classification of all circle projective structures is claimed here.''',
 r'''The two stated examples, and their covering generalization, are rigorously distinguished by the displayed argument.''',['cartan1924projective'],literal='correct',match='partial',area='Contact and projective geometry',development='annotated')
item(403,'P000665','question','The damaged global projective-structure question',
 r'''The line marked ``Problem'' contains two cancellations and survives only as a question involving ``the equation'', ``globally'' and ``locally projective space''. The scan does not recover the missing relation.''',
 r'''No unique global problem is recoverable. It might concern developing maps, holonomy, realization of a projective circle as a solution, or global equivalence of path geometries; these have different objects and answers.''',
 r'''The list of possible readings explains the uncertainty and is not a proposed reconstruction of Arnold's question.''','F',
 r'''Searches of the exact local-projective vocabulary and Cartan/Arnold sources did not resolve the deleted words. \citet{cartan1924projective} supplies context only.''',
 r'''Unidentified from the source; no open-problem or nondevelopment claim is made.''',['cartan1924projective'],literal='not determinable',match='thematic only',verification='unresolved lead',confidence='low',area='Contact and projective geometry')
item(403,'P000666','method','Removing the mixed quadratic transverse terms',
 r'''After linear straightening, \(y''=a(x)y^2+b(x)yy'+c(x)y'^2+\cdots\). Coordinate changes \(\widetilde x=x+yf(x,y)\), \(\widetilde y=y(1+yg(x,y))\) are said to kill \(b,c\).''',
 r'''Work locally near \(y=p=0\) with zero constant and linear transverse terms. Up to terms of total degree at least3 in \((y,p)\), use \(X=x+f(x)y\), \(Y=y+g(x)y^2\). The new quadratic coefficients are \(a+g''\), \(b+4g'-f''\), \(c+2g-2f'\). Choosing \(g=f'-c/2\) and \(f''=(2c'-b)/3\) removes the last two and leaves \[A=a+\tfrac16c''-\tfrac13b'.\] The coordinate Jacobian equals1 along the reference solution.''',
 r'''\oursComputed\ The explicit coefficient formulas, local integrations for \(f,g\), and the stated jet order are editorial calculations. No convergence of an infinite normal-form process is inferred.''','E',
 r'''The residual coefficient is \(H/12\) on the normalized solution, where \(H\) is the classical Tresse invariant reviewed in \citet[\S2.1]{kruglikov2009tresse}. This identifies the old invariant behind the notebook calculation.''',
 r'''The finite-order elimination is verified algebraically. The normalization does not itself settle all higher-order terms.''',['kruglikov2009tresse'],literal='correct',match='partial',area='Differential invariants',development='annotated')
item(403,'P000666','construction','The relative 5/2-density along a solution',
 r'''The remaining coefficient is written \(a(x)(dx)^{5/2}\), defined up to a multiplicative constant depending on the solution.''',
 r'''Let \(A\) be the residual quadratic coefficient after the preceding normalization. On an oriented projective coordinate interval, the residual transformations preserving the flat variational equation have \(X=\phi(x)\), \(Y=k\sqrt{\phi'(x)}\,y+O(y^2)\), with \(\phi\) Möbius, \(\phi'>0\), \(k\ne0\). After renormalizing mixed quadratic terms, \(\widetilde A=A/(k(\phi')^{5/2})\), so \(A(dx)^{5/2}\) is determined up to the constant factor \(k^{-1}\). For unoriented real coordinates use the corresponding density bundle.''',
 r'''\ours\ The weight is checked by the transformation of the normalized equation. The notation denotes a tensor density, not an ordinary differential form of noninteger exterior degree; its scale is not canonically fixed.''','E',
 r'''It is the solutionwise form of the classical relative invariant \(H\) in \citet[\S2.1, weights]{kruglikov2009tresse}. An exact later Arnold publication of this particular normalization was not verified; the invariant identification, rather than that unverified lead, supports classE.''',
 r'''The local relative-density statement is well defined with orientation and scale specified.''',['kruglikov2009tresse'],match='partial',confidence='medium',area='Differential invariants',development='annotated')
item(403,'P000666','claim','Why vanishing on one solution does not imply formal straightening',
 r'''The recorded theorem says that if \(a(x)(dx)^{5/2}\equiv0\), the equation can be straightened in formal series. The page has just fixed a single solution; no ``all solutions'' or cubic hypothesis is stated in the theorem line.''',
 r'''Taken along one chosen solution, the implication is false: \(y''=y^3\) has the solution \(y=0\), zero linear and quadratic transverse parts, and hence zero displayed density there, but its Tresse invariant is \(H=36y\), a nonzero formal series. It is therefore not formally point-equivalent to \(Y''=0\) near that solution. A sufficient corrected condition is the simultaneous formal vanishing of \(I,H\); in the cubic-in-\(p\) class, vanishing of \(H\) on an open set suffices because \(I=0\).''',
 r'''\ours\ This counterexample audits the strongest literal interpretation supported by the neighbouring sentences. We do not claim it disproves an unstated theorem about a family of all solutions with additional assumptions.''','E',
 r'''The obstruction is already part of classical point equivalence \citep[\S2 and \S4.1]{kruglikov2009tresse}; the notebook's abbreviated implication is not a new unresolved conjecture.''',
 r'''False under the one-solution reading. The source does not determine which extra quantifiers Arnold intended.''',['kruglikov2009tresse'],literal='false as written',match='partial',problem='GP-1976-03',area='Differential invariants',development='annotated')
item(403,'P000666','method','The normal form with a squared first derivative factor',
 r'''Further diffeomorphisms are said to lead to \(y''=y'^2A(x,y,y')\).''',
 r'''Every regular smooth scalar second-order equation admits this form locally near a chosen initial direction after a point transformation. First choose a local foliation by solutions and use its leaf label as \(y\), obtaining \(F(x,y,0)=0\). Then put \(X=\phi(x,y)\), \(Y=y\), with \(\phi_x\ne0\) and \(\phi_{xx}=F_p(x,y,0)\phi_x\). The transformed right side has both value and first \(P\)-derivative zero at \(P=0\), hence equals \(P^2A(X,Y,P)\) by smooth division.''',
 r'''\ours\ The foliation exists by the local ODE existence theorem and a transverse initial curve. The second coordinate change is obtained by integrating a linear equation for \(\phi_x\), choosing it nonzero. This proof supplies the precise local scope omitted in the notes.''','E',
 r'''This is a local coordinate normalization within the classical path-geometry equivalence problem \citep[\S\S VII--VIII]{cartan1924projective}, not a criterion for flatness.''',
 r'''The local finite normalization is verified. It does not make the arbitrary coefficient \(A\) vanish and imposes no global reduction.''',['cartan1924projective'],literal='correct',match='partial',area='Differential equations and projective geometry',development='annotated')
item(403,'P000666','theorem','Cubic slope equations form an invariant projective class',
 r'''The equation \(y''=P_3(y')\), with coefficients depending on \(x,y\), is said to be invariant under plane diffeomorphisms and is associated with R. Liouville.''',
 r'''The class \(F=A_0(x,y)+A_1(x,y)p+A_2(x,y)p^2+A_3(x,y)p^3\) is preserved by local point transformations wherever the transformed independent variable is valid. Exactly these equations describe the unparametrized geodesics of torsion-free affine connections on a surface, modulo projective equivalence \(\widehat\nabla_XY=\nabla_XY+\alpha(X)Y+\alpha(Y)X\).''',
 r'''The coefficients may vary on the surface; ``third degree'' means degree at most3. The graph-coordinate restriction excludes directions sent to vertical ones.''','E',
 r'''\citet[introduction, \S VIII no.24]{cartan1924projective} explicitly gives the cubic criterion; \citet[\S4.1]{kruglikov2009tresse} supplies a modern account of Lie and Liouville's earlier work.''',
 r'''Established projective differential geometry, already classical in1976.''',['cartan1924projective','kruglikov2009tresse'],literal='correct',area='Differential equations and projective geometry')
item(403,'P000666','construction','A vector field from a nonvanishing relative density',
 r'''The scalar-invariant discussion begins with \(v(x)=a(x)^{-2/5}\partial_x\).''',
 r'''On an oriented interval where the real density coefficient \(A\) is nowhere zero, choose a consistent fifth-root convention and set \(v=A^{-2/5}\partial_x\). Under coordinate changes this is a vector field. Rescaling the original density by a nonzero constant rescales \(v\) by a constant; at zeros of \(A\) the formula is singular and provides no smooth invariant field.''',
 r'''The nonvanishing and branch qualifications, and the residual constant ambiguity, are necessary. They prevent treating this as a globally defined absolute invariant of every equation.''','E',
 r'''This is algebra on the classical relative invariant identified in \citet[\S2.1]{kruglikov2009tresse}, not an independent classification theorem.''',
 r'''Well defined only on the stated nonvanishing locus, up to the inherited constant.''',['kruglikov2009tresse'],area='Differential invariants')
item(403,'P000666','construction','The projective scalar I2 and the adjoint quadratic form',
 r'''The notebook says that the projective group acts on two-jets by the adjoint representation and writes \(I_2=2v''v-v'^2\).''',
 r'''For a specified smooth vector field \(v(x)\partial_x\) on a projective line, \(I_2(v)=2vv''-(v')^2\) is a scalar invariant under Möbius changes. More generally, if \(X=\phi(x)\), \(V(X)=\phi'(x)v(x)\), then \[2VV_{XX}-V_X^2=2vv''-v'^2+2v^2S(\phi).\] The quadratic polynomial vector fields form \(\mathfrak{sl}_2\) under the usual bracket; evaluating their two-jet at a point gives an isomorphism, and \(I_2\) is the corresponding discriminant/Killing quadratic form up to normalization.''',
 r'''\ours\ The transformation identity proves invariance since \(S(\phi)=0\) for Möbius maps. A pointed two-jet includes a base point; the notebook's adjoint statement refers to the induced identification of its three coefficient coordinates with a quadratic projective vector field.''','E',
 r'''This is classical projective invariant theory; the Cartan framework predates the talk \citep{cartan1924projective}. The displayed identity is independently checked in the audit rather than attributed to an unread formula in a later text.''',
 r'''Correct for a specified vector field and projective coordinate changes. Its value scales by \(c^2\) under \(v\mapsto cv\), so it is not absolute for the unnormalized ODE density.''',['cartan1924projective'],match='partial',area='Differential invariants',development='annotated')
item(403,'P000666','construction','Invariant differentiation and the higher In',
 r'''The recurrence is \(I_3=vI_2'\), then \(I_n=vI_{n-1}'\).''',
 r'''The operator \(\nabla_v=v(x)d/dx\) is coordinate invariant. Starting from the Möbius scalar \(I_2\), define \(I_n=\nabla_v^{n-2}I_2\) for \(n\ge3\); each is a scalar invariant of the specified vector field on the projective line. If \(v\) is rescaled by a constant \(c\), then \(I_n\) is rescaled by \(c^n\). No completeness or functional independence of this list is claimed.''',
 r'''\ours\ Coordinate invariance follows from \(V\partial_X=v\partial_x\). Induction proves the scaling law, recording exactly the ambiguity inherited from the density.''','E',
 r'''Invariant differentiation is part of the classical differential-invariant calculus reviewed in \citet[\S1--2]{kruglikov2009tresse}. The notebook's narrower recurrence is verified directly here.''',
 r'''The recurrence is correct with its scale qualification; it is not a complete classification of second-order equations.''',['kruglikov2009tresse'],literal='correct',match='partial',area='Differential invariants',development='annotated')
item(403,'P000666','conjecture','Gelfand’s proposed connection with Gelfand–Fuchs cohomology',
 r'''The final line says \ru{Гипотеза Гельфанда: связано с когомологиями Гельфанда--Фукса}. Pommaret is then mentioned as having worked on differential invariants.''',
 r'''The source specifies neither a Lie algebra/module, a cohomological degree, a cocycle nor a map relating cohomology to the displayed invariants. Consequently no definite conjecture with verifiable quantifiers can be extracted.''',
 r'''The cohomological terminology is retained as a proposed connection. It is not automatically identified with the Virasoro cocycle, the Schwarzian cocycle or an arbitrary theorem about formal vector fields.''','F',
 r'''Targeted searches of the5/2-density, projective-vector-field invariants and Gelfand--Fuchs terminology did not identify an exact statement. \citet{kruglikov2009tresse} matches the differential-invariant side only.''',
 r'''Unidentified as a precise conjecture. No C/D classification or development claim is warranted.''',['kruglikov2009tresse'],literal='not determinable',match='thematic only',verification='unresolved lead',confidence='low',speaker='I. M. Gelfand',area='Differential invariants and cohomology')
