talk(404,'1976-09-20','S. P. Novikov','P000667-P000669','Lie superalgebras and the Wess--Zumino multiplet','talk',
 r'''Novikov explicitly recommends Ogievetsky's UFN review and a Zumino review, with Volkov and Akulov noted in the margin. He defines Lie superalgebras, uses real Majorana spinors to construct supertranslations, and records Gelfand's request to adjoin Lorentz generators. The Wess--Zumino chiral multiplet, its auxiliary fields, Lagrangian and infinitesimal transformations occupy the remainder. The material predates the talk, as the cited1975 review confirms. Several faithful notebook errors require emendation: Gol'fand's name, the scalar label on the spinor, an uncontracted kinetic index, and incompatible coefficients between the two action formulas.''',catalogue_pages='P000666-P000670')
item(404,'P000667-P000668','definition','Lie superalgebra signs and parity',
 r'''\(L=L_0\oplus L_1\), \([a,b]=(-1)^{da\,db+1}[b,a]\), and Jacobi ``with signs''. The first definition writes \(da=\dim a\), while the continuation correctly writes \(\operatorname{deg}a\).''',
 r'''Over a field of characteristic zero, a Lie superalgebra is a \(\mathbb Z/2\)-graded vector space with a bilinear degree-zero bracket, satisfying for homogeneous elements \([a,b]=-(-1)^{|a||b|}[b,a]\) and \((-1)^{|a||c|}[a,[b,c]]+(-1)^{|b||a|}[b,[c,a]]+(-1)^{|c||b|}[c,[a,b]]=0\). The number \(|a|\) is parity, not vector-space dimension.''',
 r'''The complete Jacobi formula and homogeneous-element qualification are supplied. The subsequent manuscript line decides the intended meaning of \(da\).''','E',
 r'''\citet[\S2]{ogievetskymezincescu1975superfields} gives the pre-talk algebraic framework. The seminar had already treated Lie superalgebras in1975-389.5.''',
 r'''Standard definition; the dimensional notation is a faithful local error resolved by the next page.''',['ogievetskymezincescu1975superfields'],literal='false as written',match='partial',area='Lie superalgebras')
item(404,'P000667-P000668','construction','The associative supercommutator',
 r'''Even-even and even-odd brackets are commutators; odd-odd brackets are anticommutators in an associative matrix algebra.''',
 r'''For an associative \(\mathbb Z/2\)-graded algebra \(A\) with \(A_iA_j\subset A_{i+j}\), \([a,b]_s=ab-(-1)^{|a||b|}ba\) defines a Lie superalgebra. Any graded linear subspace closed under this bracket is a Lie subsuperalgebra. A matrix example requires a chosen grading, such as the block grading of \(\operatorname{End}(V_0\oplus V_1)\). Closure under associative multiplication is not required for the subsuperalgebra.''',
 r'''The grading of the ambient associative algebra is made explicit; arbitrary ungraded matrix subspaces with an assigned parity need not work.''','E',
 r'''This standard realization is used in \citet[\S2]{ogievetskymezincescu1975superfields}; see also the earlier occurrence1975-389.5.''',
 r'''Established directly by expanding the graded Jacobi identity.''',['ogievetskymezincescu1975superfields'],literal='correct',area='Lie superalgebras')
item(404,'P000668','construction','Real Majorana spinors in signature (-,+,+,+)',
 r'''The metric is \(\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)\), the spinor space is explicitly corrected to real dimension4, and four real matrices satisfy \(\gamma_\mu\gamma_\nu+\gamma_\nu\gamma_\mu=2\eta_{\mu\nu}\). A barred spinor is written by multiplication by \(\gamma_0\).''',
 r'''For this signature, the real Clifford algebra \(\mathrm{Cl}(3,1)\) is isomorphic to \(M_4(\mathbb R)\) and acts on a four-dimensional real Majorana module \(S\). One may choose \(\gamma_0\) antisymmetric and \(\gamma_i\) symmetric. A charge-conjugation bilinear pairing must be specified when converting a column spinor to a row; for a compatible real basis one can write \(\bar s=s^{\mathsf T}C\). The covariance formulas depend on this convention.''',
 r'''The transpose and charge-conjugation matrix distinguish the algebraic pairing from an unexplained row/column product. ``Approximately quaternions'' is only an analogy, not the Clifford-algebra identification.''','E',
 r'''The Majorana conventions used for the component theory are discussed in \citet[\S3 and Appendix]{ogievetskymezincescu1975superfields}.''',
 r'''The real four-component construction is correct for the stated signature; barred-index formulas require a pairing convention.''',['ogievetskymezincescu1975superfields'],area='Spin geometry and supersymmetry')
item(404,'P000668','construction','The four-dimensional supertranslation algebra',
 r'''After crossing out ``Poincar\'e'', the even part is translations; the odd part is spinors, with \([L_0,L_1]=0\) and \([\bar Q_i,\bar Q_j]=-2(\gamma^\mu)_{ij}P_\mu\).''',
 r'''Let \(V=\mathbb R^{1,3}\) and \(S\) a real Majorana spin module. A nonzero \(\operatorname{Spin}(1,3)\)-equivariant symmetric map \(\beta:S\otimes S\to V\) defines a Lie superalgebra \(V\oplus S\) by \([s,t]=\beta(s,t)\), with \(V\) central. In a Majorana matrix formula the symmetric matrices are \(C\gamma^\mu\), with indices placed consistently. Equivalently, the complex Weyl notation has \(\{Q_\alpha,\bar Q_{\dot\beta}\}=2\sigma^\mu_{\alpha\dot\beta}P_\mu\), \(\{Q,Q\}=\{\bar Q,\bar Q\}=0\).''',
 r'''The invariant pairing and normalizations replace the notebook's unqualified two-lowered-index gamma matrix. Because odd brackets land in the central even space, the three-odd Jacobi identity is immediate.''','E',
 r'''The pre-talk construction appears in \citet[\S2]{ogievetskymezincescu1975superfields}; the1971 algebra is reproduced in \citet[Appendix, equation1]{likhtman2001supersymmetry}.''',
 r'''Established supertranslation algebra. It has four even translations and four real odd generators, before Lorentz generators are added.''',['ogievetskymezincescu1975superfields','likhtman2001supersymmetry'],match='partial',area='Lie superalgebras')
poincare_id=item(404,'P000668','problem','Extending supertranslations to the Poincaré superalgebra',
 r'''Gelfand asks to extend the Poincar\'e algebra to a superalgebra. The preceding historical sentence names ``Gelfand and Likhtman''. ''',
 r'''Adjoin \(\mathfrak{so}(1,3)\) to the supertranslation algebra by its vector action on \(V\) and spin action \(\rho\) on \(S\): \(\mathfrak s=\mathfrak{so}(1,3)\ltimes(V\oplus S)\), \([M,s]=\rho(M)s\). The \(\mathfrak{so}(1,3)\)-equivariance of \(\beta\) is exactly the mixed Jacobi condition. Thus \(\mathfrak s_0=\mathfrak{iso}(1,3)\), \(\mathfrak s_1=S\), giving the four-dimensional \(N=1\) super-Poincar\'e algebra without central charges.''',
 r'''The request is interpreted as the existence/construction problem actually written, not a classification of all possible extensions. The historical name is Yu.A.Gol'fand, not the seminar's I.M.Gelfand.''','E',
 r'''\citet[Appendix, equation1 and release note]{likhtman2001supersymmetry} reproduces a preprint passed for printing12April1971; its references identify the Gol'fand--Likhtman1971 publication. The requested extension was therefore already known before this talk.''',
 r'''Solved by the displayed semidirect construction; not a1976 open problem. The2001 reprint date is separated from the1971 circulation and publication.''',['likhtman2001supersymmetry'],literal='correct',problem='GP-1976-04',speaker='I. M. Gelfand',area='Lie superalgebras')
item(404,'P000668','definition','The off-shell Wess--Zumino chiral multiplet',
 r'''The list is \(A\) scalar, \(B\) pseudoscalar, \(\psi\) again labelled scalar, and auxiliary \(F\) scalar, \(G\) pseudoscalar. A bracket groups the five fields as a supermultiplet.''',
 r'''The four-dimensional \(N=1\) chiral multiplet comprises two real bosonic fields \(A,B\), one four-real-component Majorana fermion \(\psi\), and two real auxiliary bosons \(F,G\). Off shell it has four real bosonic and four real fermionic component functions; the auxiliary fields have no kinetic terms. The scalar label on \(\psi\) is incompatible with its gamma-matrix kinetic term and supersymmetry variations.''',
 r'''The spinor correction is compelled both by the following notebook formulas and the named model. The off-shell component count does not count propagating degrees of freedom.''','E',
 r'''\citet[\S3, chiral superfield; \S4(b)]{ogievetskymezincescu1975superfields} describes the pre-existing Wess--Zumino model, introduced in1974.''',
 r'''Established multiplet; \(\psi\) must be a spinor. No experimental discovery of a supersymmetric multiplet is implied.''',['ogievetskymezincescu1975superfields'],literal='false as written',match='partial',area='Supersymmetric field theory')
item(404,'P000668-P000669','construction','A consistent supersymmetric action and the notebook’s coefficient defects',
 r'''Two formulas are named the Wess--Zumino Lagrangian, one after and one before eliminating \(F,G\). The latter has \(\bar\Psi\gamma^\beta\partial_\mu\Psi\) and a fermion mass coefficient differing by a factor2 from the former.''',
 r'''One consistent modern convention uses metric \((+---)\), a complex scalar \(\phi\), Weyl fermion \(\chi\), complex auxiliary field \(\mathcal F\), and holomorphic \(W(\phi)=m\phi^2/2+g\phi^3/3\): \[\mathcal L=\partial_\mu\bar\phi\,\partial^\mu\phi+i\bar\chi\bar\sigma^\mu\partial_\mu\chi+\bar{\mathcal F}\mathcal F+\bigl(\mathcal F W'(\phi)-\tfrac12W''(\phi)\chi\chi+\text{h.c.}\bigr).\] Here spinor contractions use the standard antisymmetric two-spinor pairing. This action has the intended chiral supersymmetry; it is not a term-for-term endorsement of the notebook's inconsistent real-component display.''',
 r'''The complex normalization, opposite metric convention and \(W\) are expressly modern reconstruction. In the notebook, the kinetic indices must contract; moreover eliminating bosonic auxiliary fields cannot change an untouched fermion mass coefficient from \(-im\) to \(-im/2\).''','E',
 r'''The intended component construction is verified in \citet[\S4(b), equations4.12--4.23]{ogievetskymezincescu1975superfields}, already in the December1975 review.''',
 r'''The standard model is established. The literal pair of notebook Lagrangians is inconsistent and should not be used as a checked supersymmetric action.''',['ogievetskymezincescu1975superfields'],literal='false as written',match='partial',area='Supersymmetric field theory')
item(404,'P000668-P000669','method','Eliminating the auxiliary fields and auditing the potential',
 r'''Because no derivatives of \(F,G\) occur, their Euler equations are said to recover the preceding action. Its cubic scalar term is positive and its quartic coefficient is written ambiguously as \(g_2^2\).''',
 r'''Using exactly the real auxiliary terms printed on P000669, \[\mathcal L_{\rm aux}=\tfrac12(F^2+G^2)+m(FA+GB)+g\{F(A^2-B^2)+2GAB\},\] their equations uniquely give \(F=-mA-g(A^2-B^2)\), \(G=-mB-2gAB\). Substitution yields \[-\tfrac12m^2(A^2+B^2)-mgA(A^2+B^2)-\tfrac12g^2(A^2+B^2)^2.\] Thus the cubic sign in P000668 is inconsistent with P000669; \(g^2/2\) is the forced quartic coefficient in these conventions.''',
 r'''\oursComputed\ Completing both squares independently verifies the substitution. The scan has no clear fraction bar at \(g_2^2\), so the quartic change is an evidence-based emendation, not a confident new paleographic reading.''','E',
 r'''Auxiliary elimination is part of the pre-existing construction \citep[\S4(b), equations4.16 and4.23]{ogievetskymezincescu1975superfields}. The comparison here checks the notebook internally.''',
 r'''The algebraic elimination is exact. It diagnoses a faithful cubic-sign error and an ambiguous quartic notation; it does not repair all fermionic conventions automatically.''',['ogievetskymezincescu1975superfields'],literal='false as written',match='partial',area='Supersymmetric field theory',development='annotated')
item(404,'P000669','construction','Infinitesimal supersymmetry and invariance of the action',
 r'''Five variations of \(A,B,\Psi,F,G\) are displayed, with \(\delta A=i\bar\alpha\Psi\), derivative terms in \(\delta\Psi\), and derivatives of \(\Psi\) in \(\delta F,\delta G\). The text says that the Lagrangian is invariant.''',
 r'''In the consistent complex convention of the previous occurrence, take a constant Grassmann-odd Weyl parameter \(\epsilon\). The transformations \(\delta\phi=\sqrt2\epsilon\chi\), \(\delta\chi=i\sqrt2\sigma^\mu\bar\epsilon\,\partial_\mu\phi+\sqrt2\epsilon\mathcal F\), \(\delta\mathcal F=i\sqrt2\bar\epsilon\bar\sigma^\mu\partial_\mu\chi\), and their conjugates close off shell on translations. The variation of \(\mathcal L\) is a total derivative, so the action is invariant when boundary terms vanish. It is not necessary for the density to be pointwise unchanged.''',
 r'''Odd parameters, the use of an even variation operator and the total-derivative qualification are supplied. A term-by-term sign identification with the notebook's barred-Majorana conventions is not asserted.''','E',
 r'''\citet[\S1, equation1.1; \S4(b)]{ogievetskymezincescu1975superfields} verifies this pre-talk off-shell symmetry and component interpretation.''',
 r'''Established classical supersymmetry. The named action and multiplet are identified exactly, while the notebook's unexplained sign conventions receive only a partial formula match.''',['ogievetskymezincescu1975superfields'],match='partial',area='Supersymmetric field theory')
talk(405,'1976-09-20','A. A. Migdal','P000669','Why quarks suggest local colour gauge fields','talk',
 r'''Migdal's short discussion begins after Novikov's variations on P000669. It moves from three-quark nucleons and quark--antiquark mesons to short-distance freedom, confinement, relativistic locality and the colour/flavour matrix. No QCD field equation is actually written. Its force-law and uncertainty-principle slogans are not mathematical confinement proofs. The matrix uses three light flavours and three colours; it should not be interpreted as nine independent flavours or as an exhaustive1976 particle inventory. Bernstein's dated18/X-76 lecture starts only on P000670 and is outside this record.''',catalogue_pages='P000668-P000670')
item(405,'P000669','claim','Valence quarks in nucleons and mesons',
 r'''A proton is drawn with three dots labelled ``3quarks''; the last sentence says mesons are made from a quark and an antiquark.''',
 r'''In the minimal valence-quark model, a proton has flavour content \(uud\), a neutron \(udd\), and ordinary mesons have \(q\bar q\) content. Colour singlets occur through \(\Lambda^3\mathbb C^3\cong\mathbb C\) for three quarks and the contraction \(\mathbb C^3\otimes(\mathbb C^3)^*\to\mathbb C\) for a quark--antiquark pair. These are valence classifications, not assertions that interacting hadrons contain exactly this number of quantum excitations.''',
 r'''The representation-theoretic singlet maps make the claim precise; gluons, sea pairs and nonminimal hadrons are not excluded.''','E',
 r'''The pre-talk constituent picture is recalled by \citet[\S I]{wilson1974confinement}; the modern qualification is explicit in \citet[\S15.1]{amslercredegrand2024quark}. It continues1975-377.1.''',
 r'''The valence terminology remains standard with the stated limits; it is not an exact Fock-space decomposition theorem.''',['wilson1974confinement','amslercredegrand2024quark'],area='Quantum field theory and quark structure')
item(405,'P000669','claim','Short-distance freedom seen through electromagnetic probes',
 r'''Quarks are said to be ``free inside'' and visible when the proton is probed by gamma quanta.''',
 r'''The controlled theoretical statement is asymptotic freedom: for four-dimensional \(SU(3)\) gauge theory with \(N_f\) Dirac fermions in the fundamental representation and no additional scalar matter, \[\mu\frac{dg}{d\mu}=-\frac{11-2N_f/3}{16\pi^2}g^3+O(g^5).\] For \(N_f\le16\), the weak-coupling ultraviolet flow is toward \(g=0\). Deep-inelastic electromagnetic scattering probes short-distance charged constituents, with calculable scaling violations; it does not show exactly free quarks at all distances inside a hadron.''',
 r'''The beta-function formula and regime are supplied as the precise interpretation of the physical shorthand, not as a computation performed at this talk.''','E',
 r'''\citet[equations8--9]{grosswilczek1973ultraviolet} published the result25June1973, received27April1973, before the lecture.''',
 r'''Established perturbative ultraviolet behaviour. It does not by itself prove long-distance confinement.''',['grosswilczek1973ultraviolet'],match='partial',area='Quantum gauge theory')
item(405,'P000669','claim','Confinement as a pre-existing physical programme',
 r'''The juxtaposed assertion is that quarks cannot be taken out of a nucleon and that the binding is associated with colour.''',
 r'''The intended physical hypothesis is absence of isolated colour-charged asymptotic particles, while observable hadron states are colour singlets. A precise theorem would additionally require a defined quantum gauge theory, its physical Hilbert space and a specified confinement criterion. Those data are not supplied by this page. Cutoff-lattice strong-coupling confinement and continuum QCD confinement are different statements.''',
 r'''This records the prior physical programme without replacing the notes by a new request to solve the continuum Yang--Mills mass-gap problem.''','E',
 r'''\citet[abstract and \S\S I--II]{wilson1974confinement} had already formulated a gauge-theoretic confinement mechanism and its lattice-cutoff limitation. \citet[\S15.1]{amslercredegrand2024quark} still phrases general QCD confinement as a belief.''',
 r'''ClassE marks pre-talk exposition, not a completed rigorous continuum proof. The notebook provides no unique additional unresolved mathematical scope to index as a new C/D problem.''',['wilson1974confinement','amslercredegrand2024quark'],literal='not determinable',match='partial',confidence='medium',area='Quantum gauge theory')
item(405,'P000669','claim','A growing potential is not necessarily a growing force',
 r'''The emphatic sentence is \ru{В теории силы должны расти с расстояниями!}''',
 r'''In a static-source model, a confining potential \(V(R)\sim\sigma R\), \(\sigma>0\), has force magnitude \(|V'(R)|\sim\sigma\), not a force diverging with separation. Formally an area law \(\langle W(R,T)\rangle\sim e^{-\sigma RT}\) yields this linear potential via \(V(R)=-\lim_{T\to\infty}T^{-1}\log\langle W(R,T)\rangle\), if that limit exists. With dynamical light quarks the asymptotic static-source problem also involves string breaking.''',
 r'''\ours\ Differentiating a linear potential suffices to refute the necessity of increasing force. The Wilson-loop limit is an explanatory reconstruction with its existence assumption explicit.''','E',
 r'''The area-law mechanism is already in \citet[\S II and \S IV]{wilson1974confinement}; modern model and string-breaking qualifications appear in \citet[\S\S15.7--15.8]{amslercredegrand2024quark}.''',
 r'''The literal force requirement is false. The appropriate statement concerns energy cost and the specified static-source regime.''',['wilson1974confinement','amslercredegrand2024quark'],literal='false as written',match='partial',area='Quantum gauge theory',development='annotated')
item(405,'P000669','remark','The uncertainty-principle slogan does not specify a stability theorem',
 r'''A short sentence says that by the uncertainty principle the quarks cannot merge.''',
 r'''An uncertainty relation such as \(\Delta x\,\Delta p\ge\hbar/2\) gives a kinetic localization cost only after a state and Hamiltonian are specified. Whether that cost prevents collapse depends on the attractive interaction, particle statistics and relativistic regime. None of these quantitative data occurs in the sentence.''',
 r'''No particular many-body stability inequality or confining Hamiltonian is inferred.''','F',
 r'''The exact stability assertion could not be identified in the searched confinement and quark-model sources; \citet[\S15.7]{amslercredegrand2024quark} discusses models but cannot supply the missing Hamiltonian.''',
 r'''Unidentified as a mathematical result. The sentence is physical intuition, not an independent proof of confinement or noncollapse.''',['amslercredegrand2024quark'],literal='not determinable',match='thematic only',verification='unresolved lead',confidence='low',area='Mathematical physics')
item(405,'P000669','method','Local Lorentz-covariant gauge dynamics instead of a prescribed force law',
 r'''Relativistic invariance is said to obstruct a direct potential law; pointlike probing is offered as evidence for locality, and the proposed response is to construct Lagrangians and gauge fields.''',
 r'''A local classical model on Minkowski space uses an \(SU(3)\) connection \(A_\mu\), its curvature \(F_{\mu\nu}\), and Dirac fields \(q_f\) in the colour fundamental representation, with \(\mathcal L=-\tfrac14F^a_{\mu\nu}F^{a\mu\nu}+\sum_f\bar q_f(i\gamma^\mu D_\mu-m_f)q_f\). This is local, Lorentz covariant and invariant under smooth local colour changes. Small observed constituent size is evidence for a useful local-field model; it is not a proof of the exact quantum microcausality axiom.''',
 r'''The explicit Lagrangian is a labelled modern reconstruction of the proposed method; no action formula appears in Migdal's short notes.''','E',
 r'''The covariant-derivative and curvature construction predates QCD: \citet[equations2--11]{yangmills1954conservation}. Its colour application is already proposed in \citet[p.1345]{grosswilczek1973ultraviolet}.''',
 r'''Established classical gauge-theory framework. Defining its full nonperturbative continuum quantum dynamics is a separate task not formulated here.''',['yangmills1954conservation','grosswilczek1973ultraviolet'],match='partial',area='Quantum gauge theory')
item(405,'P000669','construction','Colour and flavour are two different indices',
 r'''The \(3\times3\) array has columns \(p,n,\lambda\) and rows1,2,3. The vertical group is \(SU(3)\) colour, the horizontal flavour group is annotated ``unknown'', and the text says there are nine quarks.''',
 r'''For the three light flavours, write \(q^a_f\), \(a=1,2,3\), \(f=u,d,s\). This gives nine colour--flavour components before spinor indices; it does not give nine flavours. A local colour matrix acts on \(a\), while a global flavour matrix acts on \(f\). With equal light-quark masses and electromagnetism omitted, vector \(SU(3)\) flavour is a symmetry; unequal masses break it. The full modern quark inventory has six flavours, each with three colours.''',
 r'''The traditional \(p,n,\lambda\) labels are interpreted as the light-flavour triplet, not physical proton/neutron/Lambda fields in the same gauge multiplet. The ``unknown'' annotation does not specify a unique unresolved group-classification problem.''','E',
 r'''\citet[p.1345]{grosswilczek1973ultraviolet} already separates colour from global flavour symmetries. \citet[\S\S15.1--15.2,15.5.1]{amslercredegrand2024quark} gives modern terminology. The seminar had already discussed four flavours and three colours in1975-367.5.''',
 r'''The nine-component light-flavour model is identifiable. Treating it as a complete inventory in1976 would contradict the earlier seminar's four-flavour discussion; the shorthand is therefore not silently expanded into such a claim.''',['grosswilczek1973ultraviolet','amslercredegrand2024quark'],match='partial',area='Quantum field theory and quark structure')
item(405,'P000669','definition','Local colour gauge symmetry',
 r'''The last conceptual distinction says that the colour group corresponds to a much stronger symmetry, namely gauge symmetry.''',
 r'''The gauge group consists locally of smooth maps \(U:M\to SU(3)\), acting by \(q_f\mapsto Uq_f\) and, for \(D=d+A\), \(A\mapsto UAU^{-1}-(dU)U^{-1}\). Then \(F\mapsto UFU^{-1}\). Constant colour rotations form a subgroup of this local transformation group. ``Stronger'' means freedom to choose the colour frame independently at each spacetime point, with the connection compensating derivatives.''',
 r'''This specifies the mathematical content of the comparison; gauge transformations describe redundancy of field variables and are not simply an extra independent global flavour action.''','E',
 r'''\citet[isotopic gauge transformation, equations1--5]{yangmills1954conservation} gives the local construction in1954; the colour specialization predates the talk.''',
 r'''Established local gauge symmetry. No new gauge-group extension problem is posed by the sentence.''',['yangmills1954conservation'],literal='correct',area='Quantum gauge theory')
